// Rep Max questions: English Language (AQA 8700), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in provenance/ted-gcse/questions/.
{"id":"gel-001","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":1,"q":"AQA Paper 1 Question 1 changed from 2026. What does the student now do?","o":["List four facts in their own words from the opening lines","Write a short paragraph on the writer's word choices","Pick four true statements from a list of eight","Answer four multiple-choice questions about the opening lines"],"a":3,"x":"From 2026, Paper 1 Q1 is four multiple-choice questions (4 marks) on information in the opening lines. Choosing four true statements from eight is Paper 2 Q1."}
{"id":"gel-002","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":2,"q":"Extract: \"Rain hammered the station roof. Mira clutched her ticket and stared at the empty platform, waiting for a train that was already late.\" What does the extract clearly state?","o":["Mira is frightened of travelling alone","The train Mira is waiting for is late","Mira has already missed her train","Mira is waiting for a friend to arrive"],"a":1,"x":"Q1 tests information stated in the text. The late train is stated directly. Fear is only a guess, she is waiting for a train not a friend, and she has not missed it."}
{"id":"gel-003","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"The old house glared down at the children\"?","o":["Simile","Onomatopoeia","Personification","Rhetorical question"],"a":2,"x":"'Glared' gives the house a human action, so it is personification. There is no 'like' or 'as', no sound word and no question."}
{"id":"gel-004","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":2,"q":"What does Paper 1 Question 2 ask you to do?","o":["Explain how the whole extract is organised to interest the reader","Decide how far you agree with a statement about the extract","Pick out four pieces of information from the opening lines","Explain how the writer's words and phrases create effects in a given part"],"a":3,"x":"Q2 (8 marks) is about language: you comment on chosen words, phrases and techniques and their effects. Structure is Q3 and the statement is Q4."}
{"id":"gel-005","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":3,"q":"A student analyses: \"The ice-cold corridor swallowed her footsteps.\" Which comment is strongest?","o":["The writer uses a verb to describe the corridor","This sentence is descriptive and makes the reader want to read on","'Swallowed' makes the corridor seem a hungry creature, so it feels threatening","The corridor is cold and she is walking along it"],"a":2,"x":"Strong analysis picks a precise word, explains what it suggests and links it to an effect. The other comments only name a word class, praise vaguely or retell."}
{"id":"gel-006","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":2,"q":"In \"The clerk scuttled away from the desk\", what does the verb \"scuttled\" suggest?","o":["He walked slowly and calmly across the room","He is proud and wants everyone to notice him","He moves quickly and nervously, like a small frightened creature","The writer is simply naming his job"],"a":2,"x":"'Scuttled' is usually used for insects or small animals running fast, so it suggests quick, nervous, timid movement."}
{"id":"gel-007","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a structural feature a student could comment on in Paper 1 Q3?","o":["A simile comparing the sun to a coin","The verb \"stormed\" in the third line","The adjective \"dark\" in the opening line","A shift of focus from a wide view of a town to one character's face"],"a":3,"x":"Structure is about how the text is organised: shifts of focus, changes of place or time, openings and endings. Similes and single words are language features."}
{"id":"gel-008","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":2,"q":"Paper 1 Question 3 asks you to write about the structure of which part of the extract?","o":["The whole extract, from beginning to end","Only the first paragraph","One sentence chosen by the examiner","Only the final line"],"a":0,"x":"Q3 looks at the whole source: how it opens, where the focus shifts and how it ends. Q2 and Q4 look at specific parts."}
{"id":"gel-009","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":2,"q":"A story shows a girl at her bedroom window, then the garden, then the gate, then the empty road beyond. How does the focus move?","o":["From a wide landscape zooming in to one tiny detail","From the past to the present through a flashback","From one narrator's voice to another narrator's voice","Outwards from her room, further and further away"],"a":3,"x":"Window, garden, gate, road: each place is further from the girl, so the focus moves outwards. This is a structural shift of focus."}
{"id":"gel-010","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":3,"q":"An extract opens with a calm description of a village, then sentences get shorter, ending with a one-line paragraph: \"The door was open.\" How does this build tension?","o":["Calm turns to short, uneasy sentences and an ending that leaves a question","The calm opening continues to the end, keeping the reader relaxed","A long final paragraph answers every question the reader has","The writer switches to a new narrator for the last line"],"a":0,"x":"Contrast between calm and uneasy, shortening sentences and a one-line cliffhanger paragraph are structural choices that create suspense."}
{"id":"gel-011","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":1,"q":"How many marks is Paper 1 Question 4 worth?","o":["20","8","4","40"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 is the biggest reading question: 20 marks for evaluating a statement. Q2 and Q3 are 8 each, Q1 is 4 and Q5 writing is 40."}
{"id":"gel-012","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":2,"q":"In Q4 a student writes only \"I agree\" and retells the story. What is missing?","o":["A list of every technique in the whole extract","A paragraph about the writer's life and times","A judgement on the statement, with evidence and comments on methods","A summary of the answers to Q2 and Q3"],"a":2,"x":"Q4 rewards a judgement on how far you agree and/or disagree, backed by short quotations and comments on the writer's methods. Retelling earns little."}
{"id":"gel-013","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":3,"q":"Passage: \"Tom folded his arms and smiled. Then the phone rang. The smile slid from his face.\" Statement: \"In this passage, Tom is calm.\" Which response is best?","o":["Partly: he seems calm at first, but 'slid' suggests his calm draining away","I agree, because Tom smiles, which shows he is calm","I disagree, because the phone ringing is loud and sudden","I agree, because the writer uses short sentences"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 lets you agree and disagree. Tom starts calm, then 'slid' shows that calm fading. Agreeing outright ignores the change; a loud phone and short sentences say nothing about Tom's feelings."}
{"id":"gel-014","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":1,"q":"What kind of writing does Paper 1 Question 5 ask for?","o":["Descriptive or narrative writing, choosing one of two tasks","Writing a speech or letter that argues a viewpoint","Writing an article or review for a magazine","Writing a poem or a play script"],"a":0,"x":"Paper 1 Q5 is creative writing: you choose one task, such as a description or a story (from 2026 it may ask for a story opening). Arguing a viewpoint is Paper 2 Q5."}
{"id":"gel-015","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence contains a simile?","o":["The fog crept in like a patient thief.","The fog is a patient thief.","The fog stole the whole street.","Fog, fog and more fog."],"a":0,"x":"A simile compares using 'like' or 'as'. 'Is a thief' is a metaphor, 'stole the street' is personification without 'like' or 'as', and the last is repetition."}
{"id":"gel-016","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which opening drops the reader straight into the action?","o":["My name is Sam and I am going to tell you about my life.","It was a Tuesday and I had cereal for breakfast.","The window shattered before I had even reached the door.","Let me begin by describing the history of my town."],"a":2,"x":"Starting in the middle of an event grabs attention. The other openings introduce background slowly."}
{"id":"gel-017","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence shows a feeling instead of just telling the reader?","o":["She was very nervous about the interview.","She felt anxious and worried all the time.","Nervous was how she felt that day.","Her fingers drummed the table and her eyes kept flicking to the clock."],"a":3,"x":"Showing uses actions and details the reader can picture. The other sentences simply name the feeling."}
{"id":"gel-018","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":1,"q":"Paper 1 Q5 is worth 40 marks. How many are for technical accuracy (spelling, punctuation, vocabulary, sentence variety)?","o":["16","8","24","20"],"a":0,"x":"Q5 has 24 marks for content and organisation and 16 for technical accuracy."}
{"id":"gel-019","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","o":["The storm was loud, we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud, so we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud, so, we hid in the cellar."],"a":1,"x":"A comma plus a joining word such as 'so' links two main clauses. A comma alone is a comma splice, no punctuation is a run-on, and 'so' needs no comma after it."}
{"id":"gel-020","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which is a complex sentence?","o":["Although the rain had stopped, the path stayed slippery.","The rain had stopped, but the path stayed slippery.","The path stayed slippery after the rain.","Slippery and silent."],"a":0,"x":"A complex sentence has a main clause plus a subordinate clause ('Although the rain had stopped'). The second is compound, the third simple and the last a minor sentence."}
{"id":"gel-021","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which version has every word and apostrophe correct?","o":["Their going to the beach. It's a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. It's a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. Its a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. It's a long way, but there sure it will be fun."],"a":1,"x":"'They're' means they are, 'it's' means it is, and 'their' and 'there' are different words. Each wrong version has one slip."}
{"id":"gel-022","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Question 1, what must you do?","o":["Choose the one false statement from a list of five","Write four sentences about the writer's language","Answer four multiple-choice questions on the opening lines","Choose the four true statements from a list of eight"],"a":3,"x":"Paper 2 Q1 (4 marks) gives eight statements about a short part of one source, and you pick the four that are true."}
{"id":"gel-023","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":2,"q":"Source: \"The council closed the library on 3 May. Residents, mostly pensioners, protested, though one teenager said she rarely used it.\" Which statement is true?","o":["The council closed the library in June.","The library was closed on 3 May.","The teenager used the library every day.","Only teenagers protested."],"a":1,"x":"True statements are supported by what the source says. The date 3 May is stated; the other options contradict the source."}
{"id":"gel-024","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":1,"q":"What does Paper 2 Question 2 ask you to do with the two sources?","o":["Pick out facts from just one of the two sources","Analyse the writer's use of language in one of them","Infer differences (or similarities) between them, using both","Decide how far you agree with a statement about both"],"a":2,"x":"Q2 (8 marks) asks what you can infer about differences, or in some papers similarities, using evidence from both sources. Language in one source is Q3; Q4 compares perspectives."}
{"id":"gel-025","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":2,"q":"Source A: \"Gran loves the new bus route; it takes her to market in ten minutes.\" Source B: \"The new route is awful; it adds twenty minutes to my journey.\" Which sentence sums up a difference?","o":["Source A shows the route as slow, whereas Source B shows it as quick","Both sources show the route as quick and convenient","Source A shows the route as quick, whereas Source B shows it as slow","Source B mentions a market, whereas Source A does not"],"a":2,"x":"A strong Q2 line states each source's view, links them with 'whereas', and matches the evidence. A mentions the market, and only A says the route is quick."}
{"id":"gel-026","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":3,"q":"Source A: \"Every evening, gangs of teenagers swarm over the park.\" Source B: \"We meet in the park because nowhere else is free, and we take our litter home.\" Which is the best inference?","o":["A shows teenagers as responsible; B shows them as a threat","A shows teenagers as a threat; B shows them as responsible, with few choices","Both sources present teenagers as damaging the park","A says the park is clean, whereas B says it is dirty"],"a":1,"x":"'Gangs' and 'swarm' suggest menace; B's litter and lack of alternatives suggest responsibility. Good Q2 answers infer from details like this."}
{"id":"gel-027","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":1,"q":"How many marks is Paper 2 Question 3 worth?","o":["8","16","20","12"],"a":3,"x":"Paper 2 marks are Q1 4, Q2 8, Q3 12, Q4 16 and Q5 40."}
{"id":"gel-028","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"Surely nobody wants to see our park turned into a car park?\"","o":["A statistic","A rhetorical question","Personification","Onomatopoeia"],"a":1,"x":"The writer asks a question that needs no answer, to make the reader agree. There is no number, no human quality given to an object and no sound word."}
{"id":"gel-029","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":2,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"The plan is wasteful, rushed and dangerous.\"","o":["A rhetorical question","Personification","A list of three","A simile"],"a":2,"x":"Three adjectives in a row make a list of three, which gives rhythm and builds the point. There is no question, human action or 'like/as' comparison."}
{"id":"gel-030","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":2,"q":"In \"Tourists invaded our quiet village\", what does \"invaded\" suggest?","o":["The tourists arrived politely in small numbers","The writer is warmly welcoming the tourists","The village is a very large and noisy city","The tourists are like a hostile army, showing the writer's resentment"],"a":3,"x":"'Invaded' has connotations of enemy attack, so it presents the tourists as unwelcome and the writer as angry."}
{"id":"gel-031","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":1,"q":"Paper 2 Question 4 asks you to compare what?","o":["How the two writers present their viewpoints, and the methods they use","The facts that appear in both sources only","The spelling and punctuation of the two sources","How far you agree with each writer"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 (16 marks) compares the writers' perspectives on a shared topic, commenting on the methods they use, with evidence from both whole sources."}
{"id":"gel-032","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":3,"q":"Which sentence is the best start to a Paper 2 Q4 comparison?","o":["Source A is about a plan. Source B is about a plan too.","Source A opposes the plan. Source B has a headline.","Whereas A calmly opposes the plan with facts, B angrily backs it with emotion.","Source A is good and Source B is bad."],"a":2,"x":"A good comparison links both writers' perspectives (against, for) in one sentence with a comparing word like 'whereas', and comments on their methods (facts, emotive language)."}
{"id":"gel-033","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":3,"q":"A: \"Think of your child alone on a dark road at six in the morning.\" B: \"Official figures show only 2% of these journeys lead to any harm.\" Which comparison of methods is accurate?","o":["A uses a statistic to stir fear; B uses an emotive command to reassure","Both writers use statistics to stir fear","A uses a rhetorical question; B uses alliteration","A uses an emotive command to stir fear; B uses a statistic to reassure"],"a":3,"x":"A's imperative and imagery aim at emotion; B's figure aims at calm reassurance. Neither uses a question or alliteration. In Q4, comments like this support a comparison of perspectives."}
{"id":"gel-034","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which task is typical of Paper 2 Question 5?","o":["Write the opening of a story set in a forest","Write an article arguing a view on homework for a magazine","Analyse the structure of a poem","Summarise the differences between two sources"],"a":1,"x":"Paper 2 Q5 asks you to present a viewpoint in a form such as an article, letter or speech, for a stated audience."}
{"id":"gel-035","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which opening suits a formal letter urging a headteacher to change a rule?","o":["Hey Mrs P, listen up, this new rule is rubbish!","Once upon a time, a school had a rule.","Dear Mrs Patel, I am writing to urge you to reconsider the new lunch rule.","Chapter One: The Rule"],"a":2,"x":"Audience and purpose decide the register. A formal letter to a headteacher needs a polite greeting and a clear statement of purpose."}
{"id":"gel-036","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence best uses a counter-argument?","o":["Homework is bad. It is bad. Homework is bad.","Some say homework builds discipline; however, tired pupils learn far less.","Everyone knows I am right and nobody can disagree.","I think homework because I do."],"a":1,"x":"A counter-argument acknowledges another view and then answers it, which makes a persuasive case more convincing."}
{"id":"gel-037","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Q5 (40 marks), how many marks are for content and organisation?","o":["24","16","12","30"],"a":0,"x":"Content and organisation carry 24 marks; technical accuracy carries 16."}
{"id":"gel-038","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?","o":["The road was empty; and the shops were dark.","Because the road was empty; the shops were dark.","The road; was empty and the shops were dark.","The road was empty; the shops were dark."],"a":3,"x":"A semicolon links two complete, closely related main clauses. It is not used with 'and' after it, after a subordinate clause or in the middle of a clause."}
{"id":"gel-039","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":2,"q":"A speech refers to books belonging to several pupils. Which is correct?","o":["the pupil's books","the pupils' books","the pupils book's","the pupils's books"],"a":1,"x":"For a plural noun ending in s, the apostrophe goes after the s. 'Pupil's' would mean one pupil."}
{"id":"gel-040","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":3,"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","o":["However the council refused, so the residents, who were furious marched.","However, the council refused so, the residents who were furious, marched.","However, the council refused, so the residents who, were furious, marched.","However, the council refused, so the residents, who were furious, marched."],"a":3,"x":"The opening 'However' needs a comma, a comma comes before 'so' joining the clauses, and the extra detail 'who were furious' needs a comma on both sides."}
{"id":"gel-041","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":1,"q":"How is the Spoken Language endorsement in GCSE English Language assessed?","o":["As 20% of the GCSE grade, marked by an examiner","In Paper 1, as an extra written question","By your teacher, reported as Pass, Merit or Distinction, separate from the grade","Graded 9 to 1 like the written papers"],"a":2,"x":"Spoken Language is a teacher-assessed talk reported separately as Pass, Merit or Distinction (or Not classified). It does not count towards the 9-1 grade."}
{"id":"gel-042","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best way to plan a prepared talk?","o":["Write a script and read every word without looking up","Memorise a list of long words and drop them in anywhere","Choose a topic that interests you and plan a clear opening, middle and ending","Cram in as many facts as possible, whatever the topic"],"a":2,"x":"A prepared talk is judged on content, organisation and delivery for an audience. Clear structure and real interest in the topic help."}
{"id":"gel-043","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":2,"q":"A classmate asks an unexpected question after your talk. What is the best response?","o":["Listen carefully, pause to think, then answer clearly with detail or an example","Say \"dunno\" and look at your notes","Repeat the start of your talk word for word","Answer a different question you had prepared"],"a":0,"x":"Responding to questions is assessed. Listening, taking a moment and giving a clear, detailed answer shows the skill."}
{"id":"gel-044","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence is in Standard English?","o":["We was not at the meeting.","We weren't never at the meeting.","Us were not at the meeting.","We were not at the meeting."],"a":3,"x":"Standard English uses 'we were' and avoids double negatives and 'us' as a subject."}
{"id":"gel-045","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":2,"q":"Which phrase suits the start of a formal talk?","o":["Right, so basically, like, this is a thing.","I would like to begin by explaining why this issue matters.","Oi, listen up, I'm gonna tell ya something.","Soz, I'm not ready but I'll try anyway."],"a":1,"x":"A formal setting calls for clear Standard English and a confident, polite tone, not slang or filler words."}
